  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Project Proposal"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-info\">The intent of this proposal is for you to share your chosen data file(s) with your instructor and provide general information on your goals for the EDA project.</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\"><b>Step 1 (2 pts)</b>: Give a brief <i><u>description</u></i> of the source(s) of your data and include a <i><u>direct link</u></i> to your data.</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### <b> SD Response </b>:\n",
    "**Data Source:** City of Austin Finance Online eCheckbook\n",
    "\n",
    "For my exploratory data analysis (\"EDA\") project, I will be using a dataset published and maintained by the City of Austin, Texas ('the City'). The data was obtained from their official open data portal. The dataset is a flat csv file that represents all expenditure transactions processed through the City's financial system. Each row is an individual line item pertaining to a vendor payment (either through an electronic fund transfer or a check). The data has various dimensions like department that paid for the service, which fund they used, the vendor's name, when the payment was made etc. \n",
    "\n",
    "I would like to highlight a key accounting concept here: The dataset captures all spends by the City (operating expenses, capital expenditure, debt service payments, and even inter-fund transfers). This distinction is helpful as it allows us to understand what kind of methodology is required to analyze certain types of spends, i.e., their accounting nature. \n",
    "\n",
    "The original dataset is a vast and contain over 2 million records, as it spans a period from 2009 through April 2026. For the purpose of my EDA, I plan to use FY 2020-2025 (~624K records) to make it more concise and to also focus on a relevant and recent time window. Further, this time period was during the COVID-19 pandemic that had far-reaching consequences on every aspect of our lives.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Link to the Dataset:** [https://data.austintexas.gov/Budget-and-Finance/Austin-Finance-Online-eCheckbook/8c6z-qnmj](https://data.austintexas.gov/Budget-and-Finance/Austin-Finance-Online-eCheckbook/8c6z-qnmj)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\"><b>Step 2 (4 pts)</b>: Briefly explain why you chose this data. What about the data interests you?  Which specific fields will be the focus of your analysis (list at least 3)?</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### <b> SD Response </b>:\n",
    "I work with financial transaction data daily and most of my time is spent on analyzing this spending for variances and designing sense checks around them. I feel this dataset is not only representative of my role (very similar to a general ledger) but also is a fantastic opportunity for me to genuinely apply Python data analysis to a highly manual process. \n",
    "\n",
    "Further, the data is extremely granular too, i.e., making sense of it in its untransformed form is difficult. This makes performing an EDA on this data non-optional. Through the EDA, I hope to uncover spending patterns and vendor concentration risks that can be presented to leadership to drive business decisions. \n",
    "\n",
    "I intentionally included the years FY 2020-2025 to understand how the City's spending changed during and after COVID-19. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Fields of focus (at least 3):**\n",
    "I plan to focus on the following fields in my analysis:\n",
    "1. **AMOUNT** - This field represents the dollar value of a transaction and will be the main measure to analyze spending. I plan to understand if they contain any outliers (massive penalties, lawsuit payouts etc.) and trend identification (did healthcare spike during COVID-19?).\n",
    "2. **DEPT_NM** (Department Name) - This field identifies which city department spent the fund (e.g., Austin Energy, Police, Fire). This will help me understand how each department spends its funds and also helps me compare spending between departments.\n",
    "3. **OCAT_NM** (Object Category Name) - This field categorizes the spend type (Contractuals, Commodities, Personnel, etc.). This will help me analyze where the City spends the most, how it varies across departments, and how it has changed over time.\n",
    "4. **CHK_EFT_ISS_DT** (Payment Date) - This field shows when the payment was made. Using this field will help me perform a time series analysis of spending, seasonality, and year-over-year trends.\n",
    "5. **LGL_NM** (Vendor Legal Name) - This field shows who got paid, when they got paid, and why. This will help me analyze the aforementioned vendor concentration risk and also identify top suppliers."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\"><b>Step 3 (2 pts)</b>: Provide a brief overview of your goals as it relates to your analysis plans. </div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### SD Response:\n",
    "My goals for this EDA is to understand spending patterns of a large government/municipal organization to analyze spending trends, identify outlier/anomalies, and check for relationships within the data. Specifically, I would like to:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Departmental Spending Analysis** - Understand which department spends the most and in which situations. This is important as we can use the insights to create spending profiles and establish more robust internal controls. I plan to perform this analysis by examining trends across time, point-of-time magnitude and composition etc.\n",
    "2. **Category Trends** - Analyze changes to the spending mix across categories (Contractuals, Commodities, Personnel, etc.) for the entire City over time. This is important as it can improve the budgeting process by providing stakeholders with insights on where the spend is booked and helping them improve bases for projections. \n",
    "3. **Vendor Concentration** - Examine if spending is concentrated across a group of vendors by identifying vendors by spend. This is important as it will help stakeholders create vendor-specific risk profiles, especially to avoid undue influence on the City.\n",
    "4. **Seasonality & Temporal Patterns** - Explore if there is a degree of predictability in any of the spending archetypes (certain vendors, departments, or categories) on a monthly, quarterly, or annual scale (e.g., fiscal year-end spending surges). This is important as it can help the city deploy its excess funds more effectively and also maintain high fund balances in anticipation of spending spikes.\n",
    "5. **Outlier Detection** - Identify if there are unusually large or uncommon transactions (massive penalties, lawsuit payouts etc.). This will help stakeholders to understand if the City requires more comprehensive approval processes for these transaction types. \n",
    "\n",
    "These goals are aligned with the types of financial analysis commonly performed in standard finance roles in the industry. Understanding financial data is the first step to controlling them more effectively and I hope to provide stakeholders with more insights into the City's spending. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\"><b>Step 4 (1 pt)</b>: Read the data into this notebook.  If you have more than one file, you need to repeat this step for each file. Use a different cell for each file.</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Importing pandas as pd to begin data read and analysis operations\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "pd.set_option('display.max_columns', None)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Reading the spending data into this notebook and assigning it to a variable\n",
    "austin_spending_data = pd.read_csv(\n",
    "    r\"austin_echeckbook_fy2020_2025.csv\",\n",
    "    dtype={'OBJ_CD': str, 'COMM_CD': str, 'PER_CD': str, 'DEPT_CD': str, \n",
    "           'OCAT_CD': str, 'RFED_DOC_DEPT_CD': str, 'RFED_VEND_LN_NO': str,\n",
    "           'RFED_COMM_LN_NO': str, 'RFED_ACTG_LN_NO': str, 'RF_DOC_DEPT_CD': str},  # These are immutable codes, even if they look like numbers\n",
    "    parse_dates=['CHK_EFT_ISS_DT'],          # Parsing date on read to be allow pandas to set date field to date type\n",
    "    low_memory=False # Allowing the code to run without triggering an alert for high memory usage\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\"><b>Step 5 (3 pts)</b>: Inspect the data using the <b>info(&nbsp;)</b>, <b>head(&nbsp;)</b>, and <b>tail(&nbsp;)</b> methods.  Make sure your header is showing accurately as you learned in the Module 4 python homework.  Repeat for as many files as you have and use a new cell for each file.</div>"
   ]
  },
