  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Importing visualization libraries\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from matplotlib.ticker import FuncFormatter\n",
    "import seaborn as sns"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### <b> SD Response </b>:\n",
    "Since I work with financial data constantly, a lot of my work involves analyzing massive datasets of data for variances and sense checks. This is a realistic analog of my day job but is also an opportunity to learn how to apply data science to a realistic analysis and then imbibe the learnings in my professional capacity. Further, I genuinely understand this problem space so I can critically evaluate the outputs of my code. Essentially, this EDA will not be mechanical in any sense. \n",
    "\n",
    "*What I hope to learn from this data:*\n",
    "1. Temporal patterns - I wish to understand how Austin's spending evolved during and after the COVID-19 pandemic. I expect to find shifts in spending composition (e.g., more spending on healthcare in 2020-2021 period and lower capital expenditure)\n",
    "2. Concentration - I want to understand if there is a small number of factors where spending is focused (e.g., particular departments or vendors) as it could pose a risk to the City's financials. Plus, I will explore if that mix has changed over time. \n",
    "3. Seasonal patterns - I want to find if spending slows down and speeds up in certain time frames and if yes, where does it happen. Drawing from experience, I want to confirm if they are applying the \"use it or lose it\" budgeting methodology. \n",
    "4. Outliers - I want to explore the data in detail to define and detect outliers. This is a subjective exercise but I can apply my professional judgement to spot unusual transactions. \n",
    "\n",
    "*My hunch:*\n",
    "\n",
    "I think Austin Energy and Austin Water will be the largest spenders (beyond debt servicing) as utility entities are spend-intensive.  Also, I expect Contractuals will be the largest spend category as cities usually rely on third-party providers for most goods and services. Lastly, I expect the COVID-19 related spend patterns to jump out at me, especially within the Austin Public Health department for tests and medical supplies, with a decrease in later years. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\"><b>Step 8 (2 pts)</b>: Discuss the popluation and the sample:<br>\n",
    "    \n",
    "1. What is the population being represented by the data you’ve chosen? \n",
    "2. What is the total sample size? Does it represent the entire population or is it a subset of data?\n",
    "3. Explain anything particular about your data - example: was it a random sample or were sampling weights used with your data?\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### <b> SD Response </b>:\n",
    "1. **Population**: The population being represented by my chosen data is a complete listing of the City of Austin's vendor payment transactions (its eCheckbook) since the system's inception in 2009. It covers all disbursements made by check, electronic fund transfer etc. and spans various departments, funds, object categories. Each transaction is either operating (procuring short-lived goods and services) or capital expenditure (long-lived). The full dataset has over 2 million records spanning FY2009 to FY2025. \n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Sample Size**: Considering the vastness of the full dataset and computing limitations of a personal laptop, I have filtered the dataset to FY 2020 - 2025, which gives us a working or sample size of 624K records. This is not a random sample but rather than an intentional subset of the population, which is scoped in based on a time range. The rationale, as explained before, is:\n",
    "- To analyze and uncover trends from a period of significant disruption (economic and otherwise) and the recovery period that followed. My intuition says that will be analytically richer than other time periods, which were far more stable. \n",
    "- To analyze a dataset that is small enough to be loaded into memory but also large enough to allow a thorough EDA. Further, older fiscal years can have issues like system migration errors, legacy accounting techniques etc. that I would like to avoid in my analysis. \n",
    "\n",
    "Within this time window, the dataset is complete as it covers the whole fiscal year. \n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Sampling Details**: I did not perform any random sampling nor did I use sampling weights. All 624K records are actual financial transactions that were recorded by the City in their accounting system. I also did not use any stratification or surveying mechanism. \n",
    "\n",
    "**CAVEAT:** The dataset contains both paid and outstanding line items but in the accrual-based accounting system that is generally accepted across the world, we record entries as we incur the spend and not only at payment time. I will retain both types as they are both valid. "
   ]
  },
