    "    display(austin_spending_data[col].describe())\n",
    "    fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
    "    if austin_spending_data[col].nunique() <= 12:\n",
    "        austin_spending_data[col].value_counts().sort_index().plot(kind='bar', title=col, ax=ax)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        austin_spending_data[col].plot(kind='hist', bins=30, title=col, ax=ax)\n",
    "    ax.bar_label(ax.containers[0])\n",
    "    plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### **Numeric Fields Inspection Findings**\n",
    "\n",
    "I programmatically inspected the three numeric fields `FY_DC` (the fiscal year pertaining to the transaction), `CAL_YEAR` & `CAL_MONTH` (the calendar year and month where the entries were booked) to understand how the numeric fields were being used. As noted before, numeric-looking fields like `PER_CD`, `RFED_VEND_LN_NO`, `RFED_COMM_LN_NO`, and `RFED_ACTG_LN_NO` were loaded as strings as they are immutable and should not be evaluated as numeric fields. But, I will evaluate them as categorical fields. \n",
    "\n",
    "- `FY_DC`: All six fiscal years are present and range 93K - 121K transactions per year. But, FY 2024 and 2025 have notably higher transaction counts which are driving the average upward. This trend will be investigated in Phase 2. \n",
    "- `CAL_YEAR`: The 2019 calendar year has 26K records, far lower than other years as the fiscal year for the City starts in Oct. This means that only 3 months of 2019 (Oct to Dec) falls within our time window. Further, calendar year 2025 also has only 88K records since it has only 9 months of 2025. Beyond this, we see a spike in the number of entries passed in 2024 (121K or ~20% higher than observed mean from calendar years 2020 to 2023). I will evaluate this in more detail in Phase 2. \n",
    "- `CAL_MONTH`: The transaction count for each calendar month within the fiscal year is within the 44K to 52K range in most cases. However, months 5, 10, 3, & 9 have much higher transaction counts (27K to 61K). While the spike in September & October (month 9 & 10) makes sense due to year-end and beginning of the year accounting activity, other months warrant further investigation in Phase 2. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "==== Check / EFT Issue Date: Inspection ====\n",
      "Earliest date: 2019-10-01 00:00:00\n",
      "Latest date:   2025-09-30 00:00:00\n",
      "Null dates:    0\n",
      "\n",
      "Dates outside FY2020–FY2025 window: 0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Date field inspection\n",
    "print(\"==== Check / EFT Issue Date: Inspection ====\")\n",
    "print(f\"Earliest date: {austin_spending_data['CHK_EFT_ISS_DT'].min()}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Latest date:   {austin_spending_data['CHK_EFT_ISS_DT'].max()}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Null dates:    {austin_spending_data['CHK_EFT_ISS_DT'].isnull().sum()}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Checking for dates outside the expected FY 2020–2025 window\n",
    "# Austin FY 2020 begins October 1, 2019\n",
    "out_of_range = austin_spending_data[\n",
    "    (austin_spending_data['CHK_EFT_ISS_DT'] < '2019-10-01') |\n",
    "    (austin_spending_data['CHK_EFT_ISS_DT'] > '2025-09-30')\n",
    "]\n",
    "print(f\"\\nDates outside FY2020–FY2025 window: {len(out_of_range):,}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1200x500 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Plotting monthly transaction count over time\n",
    "monthly_spending_counts = austin_spending_data.set_index('CHK_EFT_ISS_DT').resample('ME').size()\n",
    "monthly_spending_counts.plot(kind='line', title='Monthly Transaction Count', figsize=(12, 5))\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Check / EFT Issue Month')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Count')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### **Check / EFT Issue Date: Inspection Findings**\n",
    "\n",
    "- My inspection of the `Check / EFT Issue Date` field shows that there are no dates beyond the expected dates (Oct 1, 2019 to Sep 30, 2025). Further, there are no null dates, which confirms that the dataset has a clean time coverage across the 623K records. \n",
    "- Looking at the line chart, it is clear that check / EFT issuances are on a rising trend, increasing from 6K-9K per month to ~11K by FY2025. This indicates that the number of issuances has increased over time which could be due to increase in operations, changes to payment practices etc.\n",
    "- There is a visible dip in activity in early 2021, 2022, and 2023 that may be worth exploring more in Phase 2. There is also recurring volatility throughout the dataset which is consistent with the seasonality we saw in the `CAL_MONTH` analysis. "
   ]
  },
