   "source": [
    "# Categorical Fields\n",
    "categorical_cols = austin_spending_data.select_dtypes(include='str').columns.tolist()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Looping for value counts for all categorical columns. If >10 unique values, I will be displaying top 10 categories and summarizing the rest into an 'Others' category\n",
    "for col in categorical_cols:\n",
    "    counts = austin_spending_data[col].value_counts()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if counts.shape[0] <= 10:\n",
    "        df = counts.to_frame()\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        top10 = counts.head(10)\n",
    "        others = pd.Series({'Others': counts.iloc[10:].sum()}, name=col)\n",
    "        df = pd.concat([top10, others]).to_frame()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    df.index.name = f\"{col} Category\"\n",
    "    df.columns = ['Count']\n",
    "    print(f\"{col} has {austin_spending_data[col].nunique()} unique values\")\n",
    "    display(df)\n",
    "    print()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### **Categorical Fields - Inspection Findings**\n",
    "\n",
    "**Organizational Hierarchy (`DEPT_CD`/`DEPT_NM`, `FUND_CD`/`FUND_NM`, `DIV_CD`/`DIV_NM`, `GP_CD`/`GP_NM`):** \n",
    "- Each of these fields has a code or name pair that carries identical information. I will be dropping these fields and retain the name fields since they are more readable. \n",
    "- As expected, Austin Water and Austin Energy have 208K transactions between them (over a third of the sample).\n",
    "- `Others` in `DEPT_NM` has 208K records, which tells us that there are many departments beyond the top 10. This will be important to understand more in the next phases. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Spend Classification (`OCAT_CD`/`OCAT_NM`, `OBJ_CD`/`OBJ_NM`):**\n",
    "- `OCAT_NM` has only 5 unique values. `Contractuals` and `Commodities` both account for ~99% of transactions. Some others like `Non CIP Capital` are negligible but they may have high transaction amounts. I will explore these more in Phase 2. \n",
    "- `OBJ_NM` has ~360 uniques values but a summary check of top entries (services, inventory purchases) shows us that the spend may be typical of government-spending. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Vendor Fields (`LGL_NM`, `VEND_CUST_CD`, `VEND_CUST_IND`):**\n",
    "- `LGL_NM` has 30K+ unique values and the top 10 vendors account for ~10% of transactions. This tells us that the vendor base is fragmented. I will be analyzing the vendors based on amount totals rather than transaction counts. \n",
    "- `VEND_CUST_IND` is entirely populated with `V` (Vendor), which means that the sample does not contain any customer transactions. This tells us that all records represent vendor payments. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Check Status (`CVL_CHK_STA_DV`):**\n",
    "- The dataset mostly consists of `Paid` transactions (606K or 97% of sample). `Outstanding` transactions are ~3% (17803 records), which I expect to pertain to recently issued checks. I will check if there are any unusually old checks or EFTs that are outstanding and explore reasoning as valid. \n",
    "- The dataset also has 178 `Escheat` transactions. According to a cursory Google search, Escheat (or escheatment) refers to the formal process of transferring abandoned or unclaimed property (e.g., uncashed checks). I will be analyzing these transactions to understand if there is a certain archetype that contributes heavily to these transactions. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Reference Document Fields (`RFED_DOC_CD`, `RFED_DOC_ID`, `RF_DOC_CD`, `RF_DOC_ID`):**\n",
    "- `RFED_DOC_CD` shows us that PRM (Payment Requests-Matching. 68%) is the most frequently appearing category of transaction. This value, along with others like GAX and PRC, are system codes to identify what kind of transaction is being booked and does not carry much analytical value. \n",
    "- `RFED_DOC_ID` and `RF_DOC_ID` are unique transaction identifiers with very high cardinality. They serve as document reference keys and carry no analytical value for this analysis. These fields will be dropped in Step 11.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Line Number Fields (`RFED_VEND_LN_NO`, `RFED_COMM_LN_NO`, `RFED_ACTG_LN_NO`):**\n",
    "- These line numbers tell us which line it pertains to in terms of an accounting entry. For example, 623K records (99.8% of data) has `1` as the vendor line number, which tells us that these are single-line transactions. I will drop these in Step 11 as they are not very important for analytical purposes.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Accounting Line Description (`ACTG_LN_DSCR`):**\n",
    "- This is mostly a free-text field that accountants can use to describe what they spent the funds on. Only 22% of all transactions are covered by the top 10 descriptions, with the rest in `Others`. It is akin to the `OBJ_NM` and `COMM_DSCR` fields in content and will not be analyzed unless absolutely necessary to explain an abnormality. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Period Code (`PER_CD`):**\n",
    "- This tells us which accounting period it is, which is different from the calendar month since the City follows a fiscal year that is different from the calendar year. Month 1 (i.e., September) has 61K records, which aligns to our previous finding in the `CAL_MONTH` analysis. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
